I'm confused now. The main loop is actually traversed once per player
input. If the last player input didn't take any game time (eg #adjust) it
just hops down and gets some more input. Otherwise it subtracts 12 from
the player's accumulated movement points to account for the cost of the
action just taken. Then it alternates monster moves and player moves
(monsters first) until no-one has more than 12 movement points left. Then
it chalks up a new turn, and gives each monster movement points equal to
its speed, and the player 12 at normal speed, 12 or 18 if fast, 18 or 24
if very fast, reduced for encumbrance; then it checks to see if various
timeouts have happened (I think this is where you die); then finally it
checks to see if the player has 12 or more movement points. If so, it gets
more input; if not, it jumps back to doing some monster moves (which don't
alternate with player moves because the player doesn't have more than 12
movement points).

So I don't think you can have _no_ chance to act unless you are
encumbered.

I do wonder what the significance of "12 or more" versus "more than 12" is
here - can we produce some movement oddities? Probably only around the
turn we go from normal speed to Very Fast.

Each time the timeout process is called it subtracts one from the sickness
count and kills you if it is zero.

It seems to me therefore that the worst case is the one where the player
moves next to Demogorgon about to get a single move (and consistently gets
only one double move in every two) and Demogorgon starts in a
double-double-single sequence.

Monsters go first, so he stings you; TTL 20. 
You move.
He stings you again; TTL 7.
Round ends; TTL 6.
Timeouts; TTL 5.
He stings; TTL 2.
You move.
He stings; TTL 1.
You move.
Timeouts; You die.

It seems to me that it doesn't actually matter where the player was in
their move sequence for this one; they always get three moves.

If Demogorgon was unhasted, the best he could do would be to knock you
down to TTL 2 on the second round, and then you would die at the end of
the third round; that would mean four moves if you happened to get
single/double/single.

I am a bit out of my depth here.

